{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DnuqPgtArkzkQ78TzYcyKF","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (64.7KB). Full output saved to: /ard-home/.claude/projects/-Users-al--ard-work-albertcbrown-ard-01M8CKRNTSF4DDQ9XK940VJTX0/263dc662-ad4e-440c-a094-577a3fc7d8ee/tool-results/bp35kv6a9.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n// String returns the verdict's log name.\nfunc (v Verdict) String() string {\n\tif v >= 0 && int(v) < len(verdictNames) {\n\t\treturn verdictNames[v]\n\t}\n\treturn fmt.Sprintf(\"verdict(%d)\", int(v))\n}\n\n// Decision is the result for one offered candidate.\ntype Decision struct {\n\tCandidate Candidate\n\tVerdict   Verdict\n\tSlot      *Slot  // non-nil if and only if Verdict == Granted\n\tReason    string // human-readable, for logs and the status issue\n}\n\n// Held is a job already running when the pool is built (restart recovery).\ntype Held struct {\n\tCandidate Candidate\n\tJobID     string\n}\n\n// Options configures New.\ntype Options struct {\n\tMax         int              // cfg.MaxPods; values < 1 are treated as 1\n\tNow         func() time.Time // nil means time.Now\n\tLog         *slog.Logger     // nil means discard\n\tHeld        []Held           // running jobs from state; held even if they exceed Max\n\tStarveAfter time.Duration    // 0 means 24h\n\t// Limited reports when a candidate may next start because its account or\n\t// model family is out of quota: zero or a past time means it may start\n\t// now. nil disables the check. A limited candidate takes no slot and\n\t// reserves no demand: it does not hold lower classes back.\n\tLimited func(c Candidate) time.Time\n\t// Budget reports whether a daily dollar or token cap forbids starting c, with the\n\t// reason. nil disables the check. A refused candidate takes no slot and\n\t// reserves no demand; a job already held is never affected.\n\tBudget func(c Candidate) (denied bool, reason string)\n}\n\n// Pool is the in-memory slot arbiter. One mutex guards all state; there are\n// no goroutines or timers, and demand expiry is evaluated lazily.\ntype Pool struct {\n\tmu          sync.Mutex\n\tmax         int\n\tnow         func() time.Time\n\tlog         *slog.Logger\n\tstarveAfter time.Duration\n\tlimited     func(c Candidate) time.Time\n\tbudget      func(c Candidate) (bool, string)\n\theld        map[string]*Slot\n\tdemand      map[string]*loopDemand\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"748a3d80-7d7b-4b04-ada8-d4203eb53dab","uuid":"de238453-4035-4812-a545-1ceaa7145eb8","timestamp":"2026-11-26T19:02:05.591Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"// String returns the verdict's log name.\nfunc (v Verdict) String() string {\n\tif v >= 0 && int(v) < len(verdictNames) {\n\t\treturn verdictNames[v]\n\t}\n\treturn fmt.Sprintf(\"verdict(%d)\", int(v))\n}\n\n// Decision is the result for one offered candidate.\ntype Decision struct {\n\tCandidate Candidate\n\tVerdict   Verdict\n\tSlot      *Slot  // non-nil if and only if Verdict == Granted\n\tReason    string // human-readable, for logs and the status issue\n}\n\n// Held is a job already running when the pool is built (restart recovery).\ntype Held struct {\n\tCandidate Candidate\n\tJobID     string\n}\n\n// Options configures New.\ntype Options struct {\n\tMax         int              // cfg.MaxPods; values < 1 are treated as 1\n\tNow         func() time.Time // nil means time.Now\n\tLog         *slog.Logger     // nil means discard\n\tHeld        []Held           // running jobs from state; held even if they exceed Max\n\tStarveAfter time.Duration    // 0 means 24h\n\t// Limited reports when a candidate may next start because its account or\n\t// model family is out of quota: zero or a past time means it may start\n\t// now. nil disables the check. A limited candidate takes no slot and\n\t// reserves no demand: it does not hold lower classes back.\n\tLimited func(c Candidate) time.Time\n\t// Budget reports whether a daily dollar or token cap forbids starting c, with the\n\t// reason. nil disables the check. A refused candidate takes no slot and\n\t// reserves no demand; a job already held is never affected.\n\tBudget func(c Candidate) (denied bool, reason string)\n}\n\n// Pool is the in-memory slot arbiter. One mutex guards all state; there are\n// no goroutines or timers, and demand expiry is evaluated lazily.\ntype Pool struct {\n\tmu          sync.Mutex\n\tmax         int\n\tnow         func() time.Time\n\tlog         *slog.Logger\n\tstarveAfter time.Duration\n\tlimited     func(c Candidate) time.Time\n\tbudget      func(c Candidate) (bool, string)\n\theld        map[string]*Slot\n\tdemand      map[string]*loopDemand\n\tfirstDenied map[string]time.Time\n\tstarved     map[Class]bool\n\trefused     map[refusalKey]*refusal\n\tfreed       chan struct{}\n}\n\n// New builds a pool, adopting o.Held.\nfunc New(o Options) *Pool {\n\tp := &Pool{\n\t\tmax:         max(1, o.Max),\n\t\tnow:         o.Now,\n\t\tlog:         o.Log,\n\t\tstarveAfter: o.StarveAfter,\n\t\tlimited:     o.Limited,\n\t\tbudget:      o.Budget,\n\t\theld:        map[string]*Slot{},\n\t\tdemand:      map[string]*loopDemand{},\n\t\tfirstDenied: map[string]time.Time{},\n\t\tstarved:     map[Class]bool{},\n\t\trefused:     map[refusalKey]*refusal{},\n\t\tfreed:       make(chan struct{}, 1),\n\t}\n\tif p.now == nil {\n\t\tp.now = time.Now\n\t}\n\tif p.log == nil {\n\t\tp.log = slog.New(slog.NewTextHandler(io.Discard, nil))\n\t}\n\tif p.starveAfter == 0 {\n\t\tp.starveAfter = 24 * time.Hour\n\t}\n\tfor _, h := range o.Held {\n\t\tk := h.Candidate.Key()\n\t\tif p.held[k] != nil {\n\t\t\tp.log.Warn(\"pool.adopt_duplicate\", \"key\", k, \"job_id\", h.JobID)\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tp.held[k] = &Slot{pool: p, cand: h.Candidate, jobID: h.JobID, grantedAt: p.now(), adopted: true}\n\t}\n\tif len(p.held) > p.max {\n\t\tp.log.Warn(\"pool.adopt_over_max\", \"held\", len(p.held), \"max\", p.max)\n\t}\n\treturn p\n}\n\n// SetMax changes the cap. It never revokes held slots; n < 1 is treated as 1.\nfunc (p *Pool) SetMax(n int) {\n\tp.mu.Lock()\n\tdefer p.mu.Unlock()\n\told := p.max\n\tp.max = max(1, n)\n\tif p.max > old {\n\t\tp.signalLocked()\n\t}\n}\n\n// Freed returns a channel that receives when a slot is released or the cap is\n// raised. It is buffered with one place and sent to without blocking, so\n// several releases coalesce into one pending signal.\nfunc (p *Pool) Freed() <-chan struct{} { return p.freed }\n\nfunc (p *Pool) signalLocked() {\n\tselect {\n\tcase p.freed <- struct{}{}:\n\tdefault:\n\t}\n}\n\n// Lookup returns the held slot for key, for close-out and the Reaper after a restart.\nfunc (p *Pool) Lookup(key string) (*Slot, bool) {\n\tp.mu.Lock()\n\tdefer p.mu.Unlock()\n\ts, ok := p.held[key]\n\treturn s, ok\n}\n\ntype item struct {\n\tcand Candidate\n\tidx  int // index into decisions; -1 for foreign demand\n}\n\n// Offer ranks cands against held slots and other loops' live demand and\n// returns one Decision per candidate, in input order. It replaces loop's\n// demand entry, which expires ttl after this call (ttl <= 0 records none).\n// Loops must call Offer once per tick even with no candidates, so stale\n// demand clears.\nfunc (p *Pool) Offer(loop string, ttl time.Duration, cands []Candidate) []Decision {\n\tp.mu.Lock()\n\tdefer p.mu.Unlock()\n\tnow := p.now()\n\tp.purgeLocked(now)\n\n\tfree0 := max(0, p.max-len(p.held))\n\tfree := free0\n\tdecisions := make([]Decision, len(cands))\n\ttriageHeld := map[int]bool{}\n\tfor _, s := range p.held {\n\t\tif s.cand.Class == ClassTriage {\n\t\t\ttriageHeld[s.cand.Number] = true\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\t// Validation and duplicates.\n\tinBatch := map[string]bool{}\n\tvar items []item\n\tfor i, c := range cands {\n\t\tdecisions[i].Candidate = c\n\t\tif why := c.invalid(); why != \"\" {\n\t\t\tdecisions[i].Verdict, decisions[i].Reason = DeniedInvalid, why\n\t\t\tp.log.Warn(\"pool.invalid\", \"loop\", loop, \"class\", c.Class.String(), \"role\", c.Role, \"number\", c.Number, \"reason\", why)\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tk := c.Key()\n\t\tif p.held[k] != nil || inBatch[k] {\n\t\t\tdecisions[i].Verdict, decisions[i].Reason = DeniedDuplicate, \"already held or offered earlier in this batch\"\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tif p.limited != nil {\n\t\t\tif until := p.limited(c); now.Before(until) {\n\t\t\t\tdecisions[i].Verdict = DeniedLimited\n\t\t\t\tdecisions[i].Reason = \"usage limit until \" + until.UTC().Format(\"15:04\") + \" UTC\"\n\t\t\t\tp.noteRefusalLocked(c, DeniedLimited, now)\n\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\tif p.budget != nil {\n\t\t\tif denied, why := p.budget(c); denied {\n\t\t\t\tdecisions[i].Verdict, decisions[i].Reason = DeniedBudget, why\n\t\t\t\tp.noteRefusalLocked(c, DeniedBudget, now)\n\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\tinBatch[k] = true\n\t\titems = append(items, item{c, i})\n\t}\n\tfor _, c := range p.foreignLocked(loop, now, inBatch, triageHeld) {\n\t\titems = append(items, item{c, -1})\n\t}\n\tsort.Slice(items, func(a, b int) bool { return less(items[a].cand, items[b].cand) })\n\n\t// Walk the merged ranking.\n\treserved := 0\n\ttriageGranted := map[int]bool{}\n\tvar denied []Candidate\n\tcapDenied := 0\n\tfor _, it := range items {\n\t\tif it.idx < 0 {\n\t\t\tif free > 0 {\n\t\t\t\tfree--\n\t\t\t\treserved++\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\td := &decisions[it.idx]\n\t\tc := it.cand\n\t\tswitch {\n\t\tcase c.Class == ClassTriage && (triageHeld[c.Number] || triageGranted[c.Number]):\n\t\t\td.Verdict, d.Reason = DeniedIssueBusy, fmt.Sprintf(\"a triage job on #%d is already held\", c.Number)\n\t\tcase free > 0:\n\t\t\tfree--\n\t\t\ts := &Slot{pool: p, cand: c, grantedAt: now}\n\t\t\tp.held[c.Key()] = s\n\t\t\tif c.Class == ClassTriage {\n\t\t\t\ttriageGranted[c.Number] = true\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tdelete(p.firstDenied, c.Key())\n\t\t\td.Verdict, d.Slot = Granted, s\n\t\t\tp.log.Debug(\"pool.grant\", \"loop\", loop, \"key\", c.Key(), \"class\", c.Class.String(), \"role\", c.Role, \"number\", c.Number)\n\t\tcase capDenied < reserved:\n\t\t\tcapDenied++\n\t\t\td.Verdict, d.Reason = DeniedOutranked, fmt.Sprintf(\"outranked by %d higher-ranked waiting job(s)\", reserved)\n\t\t\tdenied = append(denied, c)\n\t\tdefault:\n\t\t\tcapDenied++\n\t\t\td.Verdict, d.Reason = DeniedFull, \"no free slot\"\n\t\t\tdenied = append(denied, c)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tp.setDemandLocked(loop, now, ttl, denied)\n\tp.checkStarvationLocked(now)\n\n\tif p.log.Enabled(context.Background(), slog.LevelDebug) {\n\t\tcounts := map[string]int{}\n\t\tfor _, d := range decisions {\n\t\t\tcounts[d.Verdict.String()]++\n\t\t}\n\t\tp.log.Debug(\"pool.offer\", \"loop\", loop, \"verdicts\", fmt.Sprint(counts), \"free_before\", free0, \"free_after\", max(0, p.max-len(p.held)))\n\t}\n\treturn decisions\n}\n(eval):1: no matches found: --include=*.go\n(eval):1: no matches found: --include=*.go\n133:\tAgent   string        // role > global\n214:\tAgent           string  // default agent CLI\ninternal/config/types.go:38:// Intervals holds the poll interval of every loop. Durations under one\ninternal/config/types.go:41:type Intervals struct {\ninternal/config/types.go:56:\t// before a notice is posted; default 2h, at least intervals.prwatch, at most 7d.\ninternal/config/types.go:140:\tEvery     time.Duration // default Intervals.Scanners\ninternal/config/types.go:222:\tIntervals      Intervals\ninternal/config/raw.go:27:\tintervals              rawIntervals\ninternal/config/raw.go:42:// rawIntervals keeps every interval optional so defaults can tell \"unset\"\ninternal/config/raw.go:44:type rawIntervals struct {\ninternal/config/raw.go:126:\t\tcase \"intervals\":\ninternal/config/raw.go:127:\t\t\terrs = append(errs, raw.intervals.parse(value, cfgPath, \"intervals\")...)\ninternal/config/raw.go:163:func (ri *rawIntervals) parse(n *yaml.Node, file, prefix string) Errors {\ninternal/config/raw.go:169:\t\treturn Errors{{File: file, Key: prefix, Msg: \"must be a mapping of loop intervals\"}}\ninternal/config/load.go:197:\t\trole.Every = time.Duration(res.Intervals.Scanners)\nEach triage role owns one `##` section of `plan.md` with a fixed heading (Requirements, Design, UX, Docs, Implementation; a custom triage role names its own with `plan_section`). The first writer creates the file with `# Plan for #<issue#>`. A role replaces its own section in place on a re-run, never edits another role's section (close-out enforces it), and leaves the file untouched on `blocked` or `needs-human`.\n\n**Sidecar repos are context, read-only.** A role's sidecar (for example `vpe-specs`) is cloned by the executor, not the loop: the loop passes the role's resolved sidecar (repo and ref) on the job. The executor keeps one shallow (depth 1) bare object cache per sidecar repo at `~/.ard/sidecars/<controlled-owner>/<controlled-repo>/<sidecar-owner>/<sidecar-repo>.git`, shared by every job of that controlled repo, and exports the commit into the job home as `<job home>/sidecar` (plain files: no `.git`, `chmod -R a-w` in both modes, and a `:ro` mount under Docker). The job sees it at `$ARD_SIDECAR`. `ref` is a branch, tag or full 40-character SHA (an abbreviated SHA is a config error). A SHA already in the cache needs no network; a branch or tag is re-fetched (depth 1) for every job. Whatever the ref, `Prepare` resolves it to a full commit SHA and records it on the job's handle (and in the `executor.sidecar_ready` log), so a job is reproducible. Symlinks and submodules in the sidecar are rejected and the tree is capped at 64 MiB, like config snapshots. A fetch failure (network, auth, a stale lock, which the executor clears by rebuilding the cache once) is the transient `clone_failed`; a ref or SHA that does not exist, or a repo the App cannot read, is a permanent failure that names the sidecar. A role that wants a spec changed says so in `plan.md`; editing the sidecar is out of scope for v1.\n\n## Executors and the Reaper\n\nAn executor runs one agent job somewhere and reports back; the controller does not care where. Both executors implement the same five calls: `prepare`, `start`, `status`, `logs`, `cleanup`. Prepare also removes any `Job.Scrub` paths (the job's own role file, directly under `.ard/<issue#>/`) from the fresh worktree before the agent starts. Job IDs are ULIDs minted at claim time; they name the worktree and the logs and appear as the `Ard-Job` commit trailer. The executor captures the job's stdout separately from its combined log, because the adapter parses one and the committed log comes from the other. Scanner jobs get network egress for advisory databases; an agent job's environment holds its model credential and nothing else of value, and with `network: true` (the default) it may reach the network; see the `network` setting under Configuration for what is and is not enforced.\n\nLocal-executor details: the role directory (and its `Dockerfile`) is mounted from the config snapshot at the job's SHA, not from the working tree. The job worktree is always detached at a SHA resolved from the job's ref (the executor never checks out a branch; the committer owns branch worktrees). The controller's clone is a bare repository ard owns at `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` (lowercased), with no remote, no stored credential and auto-gc off (`gc.auto=0`, `maintenance.auto=false`). `ard serve` creates it on first start and seeds it with one fetch of `main` (up to 30 min; if that fails, serve starts paused and the next start retries), and later starts reuse it. Config loads and committer lanes fetch into it, and every committer and executor worktree is cut from it (lanes under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/lanes/`). `ard serve` never writes to the operator's checkout: it reads it only for the `origin` fallback of `--repo` and the local `paused` key. To rebuild a damaged clone, stop serve, `rm -rf` it and restart. Upgrading from a version that used the checkout: ard deletes its old lane and job worktree directories without running git there, so the old `refs/ard/*` refs and the dangling worktree registrations stay in the checkout until you remove them (`git for-each-ref --format='delete %(refname)' refs/ard/ | git update-ref --stdin; git worktree prune`). Every `git worktree add`, `remove` and `prune` on the controller's clone, by the executor and the committer's lanes alike, takes one in-process lock keyed by the clone's common git directory, exclusively, because git's worktree bookkeeping is not safe under concurrent admin commands and a fetch aborts if it reads a worktree entry that is half added or removed (#411); fetches take the same lock shared (they still run in parallel), and commits and pushes take neither. Each such command also has a 30 s deadline, and cleanup runs even when the job's context is cancelled, so one hung git cannot stall every lane. Do not run `git worktree prune` (or add/remove) in `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` by hand while `ard serve` runs: the lock is per process and cannot cover a human's git. The operator's own checkout is free to use. The host keeps two files per job: `<base>.log` (stdout and stderr combined, the heartbeat source and the committed log) and `<base>.stdout` (stdout only, parsed by the adapter). A read-only job (`ReadOnly`, used by scanners) gets the worktree mounted `:ro` under Docker, or `chmod -R a-w` on the host. A scanner job may also ask for an output directory (`Job.Output`, scanner stage only; any other stage is `ErrInvalidJob`): `Prepare` creates an empty `<job home>/out` (0700), the job sees it at `$ARD_OUT` (`Handle.View.Out`; the host path in host mode, a writable bind mount at `/out` under Docker while the worktree stays `:ro`), and it is removed with the job home. It is how an LLM scanner hands back its findings file (#204); the Kubernetes executor refuses an `Output` job until its pod has such a mount (#454), so `ard serve` under `executor: k8s` skips LLM scanners without recording a run (`scanner.skip reason=executor_unsupported`) instead of failing each one at prepare. A scanner agent job is bounded by the executor's hard timeout and the role's `max_budget_usd`; the Reaper leaves scanner-stage jobs to the scanner tick. The heartbeat is the log file's mtime while the PID is alive; liveness alone is not a beat. Phase 0 local jobs do not survive a controller restart: the pipes belong to the controller, so after a restart the job reports `orphaned` (PID alive, start time verified) or `gone`, and the Reaper kills and fails it. The executor is not an effect: it never calls GitHub or commits, so jobs run normally under `--dry-run`. Sidecar auth: the sidecar fetch runs in the controller with the GitHub App installation token in a host-scoped `http.<url>.extraheader` passed through git's environment, never argv; the job receives plain files and no credential, and the cache stores no remote and no token. The App must be installed on the sidecar repo with contents:read. Under `executor: k8s` the `pre` container instead gets a per-job installation token scoped to contents:read on the job's repositories (`github.AppAuth.MintReadToken`), revoked when the job ends; a personal access token cannot be scoped down, so that executor needs GitHub App auth. Host-mode jobs can reach `~/.ard/sidecars`, but it holds no credential. Host-mode jobs run as the controller's uid: they can read the GitHub App key and `~/.config/gh` by absolute path, write the controller's clone, and undo `chmod a-w`. Host mode is allowed for every role ([#84](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/84)), but a role that reads untrusted input (every triage, worker and reviewer role, and an LLM scanner) warns loudly when it has no `Dockerfile`; deterministic scanners are exempt, and nothing warns under `executor: k8s`, where every job runs in a container. `ard config check` and the first successful config load of `ard serve` (and any role a later config adds) log one `executor.host_untrusted` WARN per such role and also write it to stderr, so it survives `--log-level`. The local executor logs the WARN again for every host-mode job of such a role and appends a `host_untrusted` row to the `events` table. **Default agent image under the local executor (#244, part of #55).** `ard serve` and `ard config check` read the host setting `ARD_AGENT_IMAGE`: a digest-pinned reference (`<name>@sha256:<64 hex>`) or a local image ID (`sha256:<hex>` from `make agent-image`); tags are refused (`executor.CheckImageRef`, `ErrImageRef`), and an invalid value stops startup. When it is set, `Prepare` runs every job that reads untrusted input and has no `Dockerfile` (triage, worker agent, test and fix steps, reviewer, and LLM scanner jobs) under Docker in that image, with the same flags and mounts as a Dockerfile role (`--cap-drop ALL`, `no-new-privileges`, worktree at `/work`, role directory `:ro`, and so on). A role's own `Dockerfile` wins. Deterministic scanners (trusted input) stay on the host. A local image is used as is; a digest that is not present is pulled. There is no fallback to host mode: `ard serve` runs `docker version` at startup and fails if Docker is down, and a job whose Docker setup fails at `Prepare` fails there. The controller's clone is not mounted, so `git` has no repository inside the container; every Docker job gets `GOFLAGS=-buildvcs=false` so `go build` does not fail on VCS stamping. Unset keeps today's behaviour. With it set, `ard config check` and `ard serve` no longer log or record `executor.host_untrusted` for roles it covers, and the hint of the warning names `ARD_AGENT_IMAGE`. Each warning lists what the job can reach, including `~/.ard` (state, config snapshots, the pause file, other jobs' worktrees and logs). The owner accepts this risk while ard runs only on owner-controlled repos, and revisits it when moving to the spark cluster, where every job runs in a container once the k8s executor (#54) lands. Until then the Gate 1 dry-run soak on spark (see Rollout and Delivery) runs `ard serve` with the local executor in a pod, and the deterministic cve scanner runs there as a host subprocess of the controller container: its role directory has no `Dockerfile`, and the `ard` image carries its pinned tools. Only deterministic scanners run in that pod: it starts `ard serve --loops scanners,stale,reaper` ([#187](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/187)), so the triage, worker and reviewer loops never run there, even though `.ard/config.yaml` on `main` has agent roles. `--loops` takes a comma-separated list of loop names (scanners, stale, triage, workers, reviewers, reviewfix, merger, janitor, reaper, budget; `budget` is the status-issue notice loop, the claim gate is always on), defaults to every loop, and rejects an unknown name with exit 2. `ard serve` and `ard config check` refuse to run on git older than 2.47.0, because the committer's fetches fail on older versions.\n\nA worker job is several executor jobs (`<id>`, `<id>t1`, `<id>f1`, ...) under one state job and one slot; the Reaper watches whichever step is stored on the job, so its heartbeat and deadline rules apply to tests too.\n\n**One pool of job slots, drained from the end.** At most `max_pods` jobs (default 3) run at once, shared by every loop. When a slot frees, the controller fills it in this order: reviewers, then workers, then triage, then scanners. Within a class, the priority labels `ard:p0`..`ard:p3` order candidates (unlabelled = P2, several = the highest), then oldest first. Priority never crosses classes: a P3 review still starts before a P0 triage job, and at most a P0 waits for work already finishing. A relabel takes effect on the next poll. Triage and workers also run one extra search per role per tick for `ard:p0`/`ard:p1`, so a P0 beyond the oldest 50 for its role is still found. Reviewers rank by the higher of the PR's and its linked issue's label; they search only PRs, so a P0 label that is only on the issue is ranked but not searched for beyond the oldest 50 (a gap we accept). A grant does not clear the `pool.starved` warning; it re-arms when the class has no wait over the threshold, and the warning names the key and priority of the oldest waiter. In dry-run the `would_claim` lines carry `priority` and come out in rank order. Finishing work in flight beats starting new work, so a busy day grows the scanner backlog, not the pile of half-done PRs. The merger and the Reaper are controller code and take no slot. Slots are granted when a loop polls and nothing is queued: a loop that found work waiting within the last two of its intervals has that demand reserved ahead of lower classes, so a freed slot would otherwise sit idle until that loop's next poll. To avoid that, a released slot (or a raised `max_pods`) wakes the loops with live demand for one tick, after a short debounce (default 2s) so a burst of releases makes one wake; those loops offer as usual, so class order and reservations still decide, and a paused controller ignores the wake. The merger, prwatch and scanners are never woken this way. Hand-offs wake the next loop too (#406): after its label effects succeed, a close-out wakes the loops that now have work (`ard:worker:<role>` wakes workers; `ard:review:<role>` reviewers; `ard:triage:<role>`, `ard:pending` and a triage `:done` label triage, whose fan-in gate adds the worker label; a review `:done` the merger; a review `:changes` reviewfix and workers), through the same debounced per-loop wake, logged as `controller.wake reason=closeout`. A failed, retried, stale or needs-human close-out wakes nothing, and dry-run wakes nothing. A human approval comes from GitHub, so ard polls for it: the approval watch is a controller loop that takes no slot and runs no agent. Every `intervals.approvals` (default and minimum 1m) it makes three searches (`ard:approved`, `ard:needs-human`, `ard:executor:failed` on open items). An approved item with no worker label wakes triage (`reason=approval`); an item that was blocked on the previous poll and no longer is wakes the loops that own it (`reason=unblocked`; the watch keeps the blocked set in memory, so a removal during a restart is left to the loops' own intervals). The woken loop does the real check: the triage gate still requires the approval to come from an allowed actor after triage finished, so a rejected approval starts no worker. A paused controller skips the watch and runs nothing on a wake. The full triage poll keeps its own interval. A scanner run that gets no slot is skipped, not queued. The pool also starts no agent job for an exhausted account or model family until its reset: a job that stops at a subscription usage limit records the limit (in the controller database, so a restart keeps it), and the pool answers `limited` to any candidate whose role runs on that account or family (a whole-account session or weekly limit blocks every role; an Opus or Sonnet limit blocks only roles on that family, and a role with no model, which runs the CLI default, is blocked by any limit). A limited candidate takes no slot and reserves no demand, so lower classes are not held back. The block ends at the reset time the CLI reported; when it reported none the first pause is 30 minutes and each further stop with no reset doubles it up to 4 hours, starting over once a job succeeds; a reset time further away than 8 days is clamped. Running jobs are never killed for a limit. Deterministic scanners and the merger use no model and are unaffected. A daily dollar cap works the same way at the same gate: the pool answers `budget` to a candidate whose role cap or the repo cap is reached, and that candidate takes no slot and reserves no demand. A job that already holds a slot keeps it and finishes.\n\n|  | Local executor | Kubernetes executor |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Workspace | `git worktree` of the controller's clone (`~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git`) under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/<job-id>`; sidecar exported into the job home | a bare Pod whose `pre` init container fetches the repo at `BaseSHA` with a per-job read-only installation token and runs the same `workspace` code as local `Prepare` into size-limited `emptyDir` volumes; the agent gets `work` with no `.git` |\n| Runtime | the role's `Dockerfile` image under Docker if it has one, else the `ARD_AGENT_IMAGE` default image when set and the role reads untrusted input, else a host subprocess; hard timeout | a bare Pod (no Job object): init `pre`, isolated `agent` container (default `ard-agent` image) under `ard job-exec`, plain container `post` that serves the result; hard timeout |\n| Heartbeat | PID alive plus log file mtime | the controller follows the agent's `pods/log` into the host log; log mtime |\n| Logs | streamed to `~/.ard/logs/<owner>/<repo>/<issue#>/` while running; committed to `.ard/<issue#>/logs/` at close-out | followed into `~/.ard/logs/...` like local logs; stdout from the tee file `out/stdout` in the pulled result; committed to `.ard/<issue#>/logs/` at close-out |\n| Labels on resources | job id in the directory name | `ard/issue`, `ard/stage`, `ard/role`, `ard/job` |\n| Good for | Phase 1, one host, fast iteration | parallelism, isolation, untrusted inputs |\n\nKubernetes-executor details (decided, design revision 3 of [#54](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/54), recorded in [#312](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/312); the work is split across later parts of #54, and #317 splits into the API layer and the executor). There is no job API on `ard serve`. **Pod shape.** `pre` fetches with the token passed through `http.extraheader` from the environment, never argv or `.git/config`; the git dir is on a `ctl` volume the agent does not mount; the role dir comes from `cfg.SHA`, so the k8s executor refuses a config without a SHA; sidecars are at pinned SHAs; the untrusted inputs and the scrub list travel in the per-job Secret, capped at 768 KiB (over the cap is a permanent failure with a named cause); `pre` copies the static `ard` binary. The `agent` container mounts only `work` and `out` and holds only its model credential via `secretKeyRef`: no GitHub token, no key, no Secret volume, no SA token. It tees stdout to `out/stdout`, enforces its own deadline, writes a done marker and reaps orphans as PID 1. `post` has its own pinned image and never runs a binary from a shared volume. It serves one route, `GET /v1/result`, on the pod IP, guarded by a preshared 32-byte `crypto/rand` key unique to the pod, mounted only in `post` and kept in memory by the controller, compared in constant time. It answers 409 until the agent has terminated and the marker exists, builds the tar in Go and never runs git, serves one request at a time, has header and idle timeouts, never logs Authorization, and exits 0 after the controller confirms or at the deadline. The transport is plain HTTP inside the cluster (the owner's choice): the key grants only this job's result, and a NetworkPolicy limits who can connect.\n\n**Serve wiring ([#318](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/318)).** `executor:` in `.ard/config.yaml` picks the executor at startup and it is fixed for the life of the process: a later config whose `executor` differs is a config error (serve pauses, the status issue says `restart ard serve`). Cluster settings are `ard serve` flags with `ARD_K8S_*` env fallbacks, set by the chart: `--k8s-namespace`, `--k8s-instance`, `--k8s-agent-image`, `--k8s-pod-image` (both images pinned by `@sha256:` digest), `--k8s-git-base` (default `https://github.com`), `--k8s-resources` (`cpu=`, `memory=`, `memory-limit=`, `ephemeral-storage=` for the agent container), `--k8s-pre-budget` (5m) and `--k8s-result-grace` (2m). With `executor: k8s`, startup fails (exit 1) if the credential is a personal access token (also under `--dry-run`), a required flag is missing, or the config sets `roles_repo` (not supported with k8s yet). If no config has loaded and the cluster flags are set, the mode is unknown and no job loops are built until restart (`serve.executor_unresolved`). At startup the `pool-adopt` hook calls the executor's `Sweep`, which deletes this repo's job pods and Secrets that no running job record owns; pods with a record go through the Reaper's normal `Orphaned` path. The Reaper maps the executor's end reasons to causes: `evicted` (pod evicted, deleted or never scheduled; transient, one automatic retry), `clone_failed` (transient), and `no_process` for a failed prep step, bad result or missing result. Inputs over the manifest cap close out as `inputs_too_large` and are not retried. Live read tokens are added to the redaction set. `ard config check` prints a note that cluster settings come from `ard serve` flags.\n\n**Controller.** `Prepare` builds the local workspace. It mints the read token, the key and the per-job Secret (owned by the Pod), and revokes the token (`DELETE /installation/token`) after `pre` terminates; tokens join the redaction set. Completion is decided only from the kubelet (`containerStatuses`, the agent's `state.terminated`), never from the agent-writable done marker. The controller then pulls the result and safe-untars it into `<id>.result`: it uses `os.Root`; accepts regular files, directories and symlinks, symlinks last; masks modes to 0644/0755; rejects hardlinks, devices, FIFOs, `..`, absolute paths, NUL, duplicates, any `.git` component","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"/ard-home/.claude/projects/-Users-al--ard-work-albertcbrown-ard-01M8CKRNTSF4DDQ9XK940VJTX0/263dc662-ad4e-440c-a094-577a3fc7d8ee/tool-results/bp35kv6a9.txt","persistedOutputSize":66288},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01DnuqPgtArkzkQ78TzYcyKF","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
