{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GvKMFMKGHBuM6pzeXLGfJ1","type":"tool_result","content":"// Lookup returns the held slot for key, for close-out and the Reaper after a restart.\nfunc (p *Pool) Lookup(key string) (*Slot, bool) {\n\tp.mu.Lock()\n\tdefer p.mu.Unlock()\n\ts, ok := p.held[key]\n\treturn s, ok\n}\n\ntype item struct {\n\tcand Candidate\n\tidx  int // index into decisions; -1 for foreign demand\n}\n\n// Offer ranks cands against held slots and other loops' live demand and\n// returns one Decision per candidate, in input order. It replaces loop's\n// demand entry, which expires ttl after this call (ttl <= 0 records none).\n// Loops must call Offer once per tick even with no candidates, so stale\n// demand clears.\nfunc (p *Pool) Offer(loop string, ttl time.Duration, cands []Candidate) []Decision {\n\tp.mu.Lock()\n\tdefer p.mu.Unlock()\n\tnow := p.now()\n\tp.purgeLocked(now)\n\n\tfree0 := max(0, p.max-len(p.held))\n\tfree := free0\n\tdecisions := make([]Decision, len(cands))\n\ttriageHeld := map[int]bool{}\n\tfor _, s := range p.held {\n\t\tif s.cand.Class == ClassTriage {\n\t\t\ttriageHeld[s.cand.Number] = true\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\t// Validation and duplicates.\n\tinBatch := map[string]bool{}\n\tvar items []item\n\tfor i, c := range cands {\n\t\tdecisions[i].Candidate = c\n\t\tif why := c.invalid(); why != \"\" {\n\t\t\tdecisions[i].Verdict, decisions[i].Reason = DeniedInvalid, why\n\t\t\tp.log.Warn(\"pool.invalid\", \"loop\", loop, \"class\", c.Class.String(), \"role\", c.Role, \"number\", c.Number, \"reason\", why)\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tk := c.Key()\n\t\tif p.held[k] != nil || inBatch[k] {\n\t\t\tdecisions[i].Verdict, decisions[i].Reason = DeniedDuplicate, \"already held or offered earlier in this batch\"\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tif p.limited != nil {\n\t\t\tif until := p.limited(c); now.Before(until) {\n\t\t\t\tdecisions[i].Verdict = DeniedLimited\n\t\t\t\tdecisions[i].Reason = \"usage limit until \" + until.UTC().Format(\"15:04\") + \" UTC\"\n\t\t\t\tp.noteRefusalLocked(c, DeniedLimited, now)\n\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\tif p.budget != nil {\n\t\t\tif denied, why := p.budget(c); denied {\n\t\t\t\tdecisions[i].Verdict, decisions[i].Reason = DeniedBudget, why\n\t\t\t\tp.noteRefusalLocked(c, DeniedBudget, now)\n\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\tinBatch[k] = true\n\t\titems = append(items, item{c, i})\n\t}\n\tfor _, c := range p.foreignLocked(loop, now, inBatch, triageHeld) {\n\t\titems = append(items, item{c, -1})\n\t}\n\tsort.Slice(items, func(a, b int) bool { return less(items[a].cand, items[b].cand) })\n\n\t// Walk the merged ranking.\n\treserved := 0\n\ttriageGranted := map[int]bool{}\n\tvar denied []Candidate\n\tcapDenied := 0\n\tfor _, it := range items {\n\t\tif it.idx < 0 {\n\t\t\tif free > 0 {\n\t\t\t\tfree--\n\t\t\t\treserved++\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\td := &decisions[it.idx]\n\t\tc := it.cand\n\t\tswitch {\n\t\tcase c.Class == ClassTriage && (triageHeld[c.Number] || triageGranted[c.Number]):\n\t\t\td.Verdict, d.Reason = DeniedIssueBusy, fmt.Sprintf(\"a triage job on #%d is already held\", c.Number)\n\t\tcase free > 0:\n\t\t\tfree--\n\t\t\ts := &Slot{pool: p, cand: c, grantedAt: now}\n\t\t\tp.held[c.Key()] = s\n\t\t\tif c.Class == ClassTriage {\n\t\t\t\ttriageGranted[c.Number] = true\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tdelete(p.firstDenied, c.Key())\n\t\t\td.Verdict, d.Slot = Granted, s\n\t\t\tp.log.Debug(\"pool.grant\", \"loop\", loop, \"key\", c.Key(), \"class\", c.Class.String(), \"role\", c.Role, \"number\", c.Number)\n\t\tcase capDenied < reserved:\n\t\t\tcapDenied++\n\t\t\td.Verdict, d.Reason = DeniedOutranked, fmt.Sprintf(\"outranked by %d higher-ranked waiting job(s)\", reserved)\n\t\t\tdenied = append(denied, c)\n\t\tdefault:\n\t\t\tcapDenied++\n\t\t\td.Verdict, d.Reason = DeniedFull, \"no free slot\"\n\t\t\tdenied = append(denied, c)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tp.setDemandLocked(loop, now, ttl, denied)\n\tp.checkStarvationLocked(now)\n\n\tif p.log.Enabled(context.Background(), slog.LevelDebug) {\n\t\tcounts := map[string]int{}\n\t\tfor _, d := range decisions {\n\t\t\tcounts[d.Verdict.String()]++\n\t\t}\n\t\tp.log.Debug(\"pool.offer\", \"loop\", loop, \"verdicts\", fmt.Sprint(counts), \"free_before\", free0, \"free_after\", max(0, p.max-len(p.held)))\n\t}\n\treturn decisions\n}\n(eval):1: no matches found: --include=*.go\n(eval):1: no matches found: --include=*.go","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"748a3d80-7d7b-4b04-ada8-d4203eb53dab","uuid":"725e4dc1-d4a1-4e48-a9ba-e08728e01781","timestamp":"2026-11-26T19:02:07.627Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"// Lookup returns the held slot for key, for close-out and the Reaper after a restart.\nfunc (p *Pool) Lookup(key string) (*Slot, bool) {\n\tp.mu.Lock()\n\tdefer p.mu.Unlock()\n\ts, ok := p.held[key]\n\treturn s, ok\n}\n\ntype item struct {\n\tcand Candidate\n\tidx  int // index into decisions; -1 for foreign demand\n}\n\n// Offer ranks cands against held slots and other loops' live demand and\n// returns one Decision per candidate, in input order. It replaces loop's\n// demand entry, which expires ttl after this call (ttl <= 0 records none).\n// Loops must call Offer once per tick even with no candidates, so stale\n// demand clears.\nfunc (p *Pool) Offer(loop string, ttl time.Duration, cands []Candidate) []Decision {\n\tp.mu.Lock()\n\tdefer p.mu.Unlock()\n\tnow := p.now()\n\tp.purgeLocked(now)\n\n\tfree0 := max(0, p.max-len(p.held))\n\tfree := free0\n\tdecisions := make([]Decision, len(cands))\n\ttriageHeld := map[int]bool{}\n\tfor _, s := range p.held {\n\t\tif s.cand.Class == ClassTriage {\n\t\t\ttriageHeld[s.cand.Number] = true\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\t// Validation and duplicates.\n\tinBatch := map[string]bool{}\n\tvar items []item\n\tfor i, c := range cands {\n\t\tdecisions[i].Candidate = c\n\t\tif why := c.invalid(); why != \"\" {\n\t\t\tdecisions[i].Verdict, decisions[i].Reason = DeniedInvalid, why\n\t\t\tp.log.Warn(\"pool.invalid\", \"loop\", loop, \"class\", c.Class.String(), \"role\", c.Role, \"number\", c.Number, \"reason\", why)\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tk := c.Key()\n\t\tif p.held[k] != nil || inBatch[k] {\n\t\t\tdecisions[i].Verdict, decisions[i].Reason = DeniedDuplicate, \"already held or offered earlier in this batch\"\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tif p.limited != nil {\n\t\t\tif until := p.limited(c); now.Before(until) {\n\t\t\t\tdecisions[i].Verdict = DeniedLimited\n\t\t\t\tdecisions[i].Reason = \"usage limit until \" + until.UTC().Format(\"15:04\") + \" UTC\"\n\t\t\t\tp.noteRefusalLocked(c, DeniedLimited, now)\n\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\tif p.budget != nil {\n\t\t\tif denied, why := p.budget(c); denied {\n\t\t\t\tdecisions[i].Verdict, decisions[i].Reason = DeniedBudget, why\n\t\t\t\tp.noteRefusalLocked(c, DeniedBudget, now)\n\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\tinBatch[k] = true\n\t\titems = append(items, item{c, i})\n\t}\n\tfor _, c := range p.foreignLocked(loop, now, inBatch, triageHeld) {\n\t\titems = append(items, item{c, -1})\n\t}\n\tsort.Slice(items, func(a, b int) bool { return less(items[a].cand, items[b].cand) })\n\n\t// Walk the merged ranking.\n\treserved := 0\n\ttriageGranted := map[int]bool{}\n\tvar denied []Candidate\n\tcapDenied := 0\n\tfor _, it := range items {\n\t\tif it.idx < 0 {\n\t\t\tif free > 0 {\n\t\t\t\tfree--\n\t\t\t\treserved++\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\td := &decisions[it.idx]\n\t\tc := it.cand\n\t\tswitch {\n\t\tcase c.Class == ClassTriage && (triageHeld[c.Number] || triageGranted[c.Number]):\n\t\t\td.Verdict, d.Reason = DeniedIssueBusy, fmt.Sprintf(\"a triage job on #%d is already held\", c.Number)\n\t\tcase free > 0:\n\t\t\tfree--\n\t\t\ts := &Slot{pool: p, cand: c, grantedAt: now}\n\t\t\tp.held[c.Key()] = s\n\t\t\tif c.Class == ClassTriage {\n\t\t\t\ttriageGranted[c.Number] = true\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tdelete(p.firstDenied, c.Key())\n\t\t\td.Verdict, d.Slot = Granted, s\n\t\t\tp.log.Debug(\"pool.grant\", \"loop\", loop, \"key\", c.Key(), \"class\", c.Class.String(), \"role\", c.Role, \"number\", c.Number)\n\t\tcase capDenied < reserved:\n\t\t\tcapDenied++\n\t\t\td.Verdict, d.Reason = DeniedOutranked, fmt.Sprintf(\"outranked by %d higher-ranked waiting job(s)\", reserved)\n\t\t\tdenied = append(denied, c)\n\t\tdefault:\n\t\t\tcapDenied++\n\t\t\td.Verdict, d.Reason = DeniedFull, \"no free slot\"\n\t\t\tdenied = append(denied, c)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tp.setDemandLocked(loop, now, ttl, denied)\n\tp.checkStarvationLocked(now)\n\n\tif p.log.Enabled(context.Background(), slog.LevelDebug) {\n\t\tcounts := map[string]int{}\n\t\tfor _, d := range decisions {\n\t\t\tcounts[d.Verdict.String()]++\n\t\t}\n\t\tp.log.Debug(\"pool.offer\", \"loop\", loop, \"verdicts\", fmt.Sprint(counts), \"free_before\", free0, \"free_after\", max(0, p.max-len(p.held)))\n\t}\n\treturn decisions\n}\n(eval):1: no matches found: --include=*.go\n(eval):1: no matches found: --include=*.go","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01GvKMFMKGHBuM6pzeXLGfJ1","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChS4Gja1Zt66BhLtbLmWH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RioMz22DhVa3c8sL8TasQ2","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"grep -n \"Budget\\|Agent \" internal/config/types.go | head; grep -n \"Intervals\\|\\\"intervals\\\"\" internal/config/raw.go internal/config/load.go internal/config/types.go internal/config/keys.go | head; grep -n \"limits\\|Limits\" docs/proposal.md | head -30; grep -n \"^lint\\|^test\" Makefile"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1263,"cache_read_input_tokens":23622,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1263},"output_tokens":34,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"748a3d80-7d7b-4b04-ada8-d4203eb53dab","uuid":"559122b3-0195-4acf-b3bf-c1e00dffe7c9","timestamp":"2026-11-26T19:02:08.276Z","request_id":"req_011ChS4GjSLDnYKLNQRzUfnE"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RioMz22DhVa3c8sL8TasQ2","type":"tool_result","content":"133:\tAgent   string        // role > global\n167:\t// Budget caps (#8): zero means unset — the job cap falls back to\n168:\t// Resolved.MaxBudgetUSD and the daily cap to Resolved.BudgetUSDPerDay.\n169:\tMaxBudgetUSD    float64\n170:\tBudgetUSDPerDay float64\n214:\tAgent           string  // default agent CLI\n216:\tBudgetUSDPerDay float64 // whole-repo daily cap; default 50\n217:\tMaxBudgetUSD    float64 // default per-job cap; default 5\n244:// RoleBudgetUSDPerDay is the role's own daily cap in USD. set is false when\n246:// only the repo-wide Resolved.BudgetUSDPerDay applies to it.\ninternal/config/raw.go:27:\tintervals              rawIntervals\ninternal/config/raw.go:42:// rawIntervals keeps every interval optional so defaults can tell \"unset\"\ninternal/config/raw.go:44:type rawIntervals struct {\ninternal/config/raw.go:126:\t\tcase \"intervals\":\ninternal/config/raw.go:127:\t\t\terrs = append(errs, raw.intervals.parse(value, cfgPath, \"intervals\")...)\ninternal/config/raw.go:163:func (ri *rawIntervals) parse(n *yaml.Node, file, prefix string) Errors {\ninternal/config/load.go:197:\t\trole.Every = time.Duration(res.Intervals.Scanners)\ninternal/config/types.go:38:// Intervals holds the poll interval of every loop. Durations under one\ninternal/config/types.go:41:type Intervals struct {\ninternal/config/types.go:140:\tEvery     time.Duration // default Intervals.Scanners\n88:**One query per role per poll.** For example: `is:open label:\"ard:triage:architect\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:running\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:done\" -label:ard:executor:failed -label:ard:needs-human -label:ard:hold`. Six triage roles every 15 minutes, plus four reviewers, a worker and the merger every 5, is about 100 searches an hour, well under GitHub's limits.\n207:**The LLM engine** (`scanner.AgentEngine`, #204) runs the role's agent as a `scanner`-stage job (Issue 0) on a read-only worktree at the scanned commit, with the role's sidecar, a writable output dir (`$ARD_OUT`) and the model credential only; the whole repository and the sidecar are untrusted input. With no model credential it asks for no slot and runs nothing. The agent writes its findings to `$ARD_OUT/findings.jsonl`, which must be a regular file (never a symlink) of at most 16 MiB; it is decoded with the strict schema above, then checked against the fingerprint grammar, and a missing file is a failed run. Usage is recorded before any outcome check, so a failed run keeps its cost. A usage-limit stop is recorded in the limits book and the job ends as limited; if the book cannot record it, the run fails instead. A timeout, a vanished process, a non-zero exit, an unsuccessful result or an invalid findings file is a failed scan whose error carries the exit code and result subtype, or line numbers, and never agent text. The engine does no redaction itself: its decoded findings pass the same pre-filing guard as every scanner's (below), so a secret hidden behind a JSON escape in the file is still caught, and only after the guard passes are `@`-mentions in titles and bodies defused (`closeout.Sanitize`: a zero-width space after `@`, comment openers escaped), so an inserted character can never split a secret pattern the guard should see.\n328:Kubernetes-executor details (decided, design revision 3 of [#54](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/54), recorded in [#312](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/312); the work is split across later parts of #54, and #317 splits into the API layer and the executor). There is no job API on `ard serve`. **Pod shape.** `pre` fetches with the token passed through `http.extraheader` from the environment, never argv or `.git/config`; the git dir is on a `ctl` volume the agent does not mount; the role dir comes from `cfg.SHA`, so the k8s executor refuses a config without a SHA; sidecars are at pinned SHAs; the untrusted inputs and the scrub list travel in the per-job Secret, capped at 768 KiB (over the cap is a permanent failure with a named cause); `pre` copies the static `ard` binary. The `agent` container mounts only `work` and `out` and holds only its model credential via `secretKeyRef`: no GitHub token, no key, no Secret volume, no SA token. It tees stdout to `out/stdout`, enforces its own deadline, writes a done marker and reaps orphans as PID 1. `post` has its own pinned image and never runs a binary from a shared volume. It serves one route, `GET /v1/result`, on the pod IP, guarded by a preshared 32-byte `crypto/rand` key unique to the pod, mounted only in `post` and kept in memory by the controller, compared in constant time. It answers 409 until the agent has terminated and the marker exists, builds the tar in Go and never runs git, serves one request at a time, has header and idle timeouts, never logs Authorization, and exits 0 after the controller confirms or at the deadline. The transport is plain HTTP inside the cluster (the owner's choice): the key grants only this job's result, and a NetworkPolicy limits who can connect.\n334:**Layout and limits.** Pod paths are `/job`, `/work`, `/role`, `/sidecar`, `/ard-config`, `/ard-out` and `/ard-bin`; the result roots are `work/` and `out/`, and stdout is `out/stdout`, outside `work/`, so it never shows in `Snapshot.Changed`. Defaults: a 2 GiB `emptyDir` per pod, a result of 512 MiB compressed and 2 GiB unpacked, 200k files, 100 MiB per file, all overridable by `ard serve` flags. **Deadlines.** `h.Deadline = Start + Timeout`; the job-exec deadline is `h.Deadline - min(2m, Timeout/10)`; the pod's `activeDeadlineSeconds` is `Timeout + UploadGrace`, where `UploadGrace` is a 2 minute result-pull grace; the key is revoked at `Deadline` plus that grace; job-exec exits 124 at its deadline, which maps to `TimedOut`. **Settings and images.** Cluster settings (jobs namespace, images, sizes) are `ard serve` flags set by the chart, not `.ard/config.yaml`; `executor` is fixed at startup, and a later config with a different value is a config error until restart. The `ard-agent` image will be public under `ghcr.io/albertcbrown/ard` and holds no secrets (an image test checks it). It carries Go, jq, yq, osv-scanner, govulncheck and golangci-lint at the repo's pins but not trufflehog (AGPL). Roles with a `Dockerfile` use it until in-cluster builds exist.\n336:**Agent CLIs plug in through adapters.** Each adapter turns (prompt file, context paths, budget) into a command line and parses the exit code and, where the CLI reports them, turns and cost. For Claude Code that is `claude -p --output-format=stream-json --verbose --restricted --safe-mode --strict-mcp-config --disable-slash-commands --permission-mode=dontAsk --settings='{\"includeGitInstructions\":false,\"disableAllHooks\":true}' --tools=… --allowed-tools=… --disallowed-tools=… --model <model> --max-budget-usd <max_budget_usd> --append-system-prompt-file <role prompt> -- <message>` (`--verbose` is required by `stream-json` in print mode). Every stage (scanner, triage, worker, review) gets the same tool profile: `--tools` and `--allowed-tools` are `Read,Glob,Grep,Edit,Write,Bash`, and `--disallowed-tools` is `Bash(git commit *),Bash(git push *),Bash(git remote *),Bash(gh *)` (decided, #79). ard does not pass `--bare`: it limits the CLI to Bash, Read and Edit, and `--tools` cannot widen it (#105). The planted-config isolation `--bare` gave comes instead from `--restricted` (only managed settings and `--settings` load), `--safe-mode` (no CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md, skills, agents, hooks, MCP servers or auto memory), `--strict-mcp-config`, `--disable-slash-commands`, and `--settings` that turns off hooks and the git status snapshot (commit subjects are third-party text). `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_BACKGROUND_TASKS=1` is set as a fixed env var (the executor allows it) so Bash cannot detach commands. The integration tests pin each of these against a worktree with planted config and must be re-run on every `TestedVersion` bump. The tool list is not a security boundary, and the deny list is advisory, because shell indirection gets around it. The real boundaries are the missing GitHub token (Docker; see the host-mode note above, #84), the committer, which commits only `.ard/<issue#>/` from a triage or review job (other edits are discarded with the worktree), and the diff guard on worker changes. An explicit tool list from the caller (`Spec.Tools`) replaces the allow list and keeps the deny list. The user message holds only fixed text and the paths of the untrusted input files. The scanner stage (an LLM scanner, #204) has no issue (`Spec.Issue` is 0) and no untrusted file list: its message says that everything it can read, the repository in the working directory and any sidecar dir, is untrusted data, and asks for findings as JSON lines in `Spec.OutputFile` (`$ARD_OUT/findings.jsonl`), which is required for that stage and refused for every other. Because the CLI's Write and Edit tools reach only the working directory and the `--add-dir` dirs, and a scanner's worktree is read-only, the adapter adds `--add-dir=<dir of OutputFile>` after the read dirs; an integration test pins that a Write elsewhere fails or is denied. Bash is not confined by it: the scanner stage keeps the standard tool profile, Bash included. The installed CLI accepts an undocumented `--max-turns`; ard does not rely on it, so a per-job dollar cap and the executor's hard timeout bound each run. Agents run with an empty per-job CLI config dir and HOME and receive exactly one model credential, passed by name by the executor and never visible to the adapter: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) or a subscription OAuth token (`CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN`, from `claude setup-token`); that rests on the environment, not on a CLI flag. `ard serve` chooses the credential from its own environment: `ARD_AGENT_AUTH` is `auto` (the default; the token if set, else the key), `oauth` or `apikey`, and an explicit mode never falls back to the other variable. Only one is ever passed, because the CLI prefers the API key and would silently bill it (pinned by an integration test). A missing credential does not stop startup, because deterministic scanners need none: `ard serve` logs one INFO line, and a job that needs one fails with a clear error. The executor creates the per-job config dir as `<job home>/.claude` (0700) in `prepare` (`Handle.View.ConfigDir`; under Docker it is a bind mount at `/ard-config`), refuses to start a job whose config dir is not that empty directory, and removes it with the job home; the Reaper's `KeepWorktree` retention keeps it too, and it holds no credential, since the credential travels only in the environment. `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBPROCESS_ENV_SCRUB=1`, which would keep the token out of Bash children, is not set (decided, #125). Residual risk: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) is inherited by Bash children; on the tested release the CLI does not pass `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` to them; and either credential can still be read from the `claude` process's own environment (`/proc/$PPID/environ` on Linux, `ps eww` on macOS). The container is the boundary: it holds no GitHub token, and the credential is scoped to the model. The scrub is off because on 2.1.328 it requires bubblewrap (and socat) plus unprivileged user namespaces; Docker blocks those under ard's flags (`--cap-drop ALL`, `no-new-privileges`, default seccomp), so every Bash call would fail closed and without bwrap the CLI refuses to start. On macOS host mode it worked and matched the `dontAsk` outcomes. It can be revisited for host mode or a loosened container. What agent Bash can read differs by mode: under Docker, only the job's own credential; in host mode, far more, because any same-uid process's environment is readable (`ps eww <pid>` on macOS, `/proc/<pid>/environ` on Linux), including `ard serve`'s own, which holds both credentials. The scrub would not close that; it is part of the host-mode risk of #84. `kimi` and `dsh-tui` both run headless with `-p`; neither has a turn cap and `kimi` reports no cost, so turns and cost are optional in the adapter result. `config.yaml` names the default CLI and any per-role override, so a role can move between CLIs without touching its prompt. The Claude adapter also reads a usage limit and token usage from the stream: a limit comes from a `rate_limit_event` with status `rejected` (its `resetsAt` is the reset time), from the CLI's own error text (`You've hit your session, weekly, Opus or Sonnet limit`) and from `rate_limit` or `billing_error` errors, and it records `usage` and `modelUsage` token counts (by input, output, cache write and cache read; per model when the result has `modelUsage`, which is preferred for accounting). That text is matched only on paths the CLI itself writes (an assistant message carrying an `error`, or an errored result with HTTP 429 or `api_error`), never in agent output, because an agent can be made to print the phrase. Only the job's own agent step and its fix steps are read for a limit: a worker's test step runs agent-written code, so its output is never classified (a printed limit line there cannot block the account). With an API key a 429 stays a transient `rate_limit` retry; only subscription (OAuth) runs have usage limits. The adapter also reads `rate_limit_info.unifiedWindows` from every `rate_limit_event` into `Result.Windows` (one reading per window type, the last winning, also when the run ends with no result). The controller records the readings for OAuth steps only, from the job's own agent and fix steps and never from a test step, including killed steps with no result; it logs them as `limits.reading`. A recording error is logged as `limits.observe_failed` and never fails the job. Nothing is gated on the readings yet (#63). The committed fixtures are scrubbed real captures where a human has made them (`testdata/CAPTURE.md`) and clearly named `synthetic_*` files elsewhere.\n455:**Limits and credentials are per `ard serve`.** `max_pods`, `budget_usd_per_day` and `max_budget_usd` apply to one process. ard does not coordinate across processes, so when a host or cluster runs more than one `ard serve`, the operator sets every serve's limits together so the total fits the host, the cluster and the model subscription. Each `ard serve` has its own GitHub credentials: a GitHub App installation, or a fine-grained PAT after #302. See [the onboarding runbook](runbooks/onboard-repo.md).\n543:- **Cluster.** The jobs namespace has Pod Security `restricted`; the job SA has no roles and no token; `enableServiceLinks: false`, non-root, drop ALL, RuntimeDefault seccomp, read-only root fs, `/tmp` on an emptyDir, `restartPolicy: Never`, images pinned by digest. The controller Role is namespaced and lists exactly the calls the executor makes: pods create/get/list/delete, pods/log get, secrets create/delete; no watch (status is polled), no events, no pods/exec, no Secret reads. The controller gets a projected API token that expires after an hour; the chart creates the namespace, its policies, quota and limits, the Role and the admission policy only with `executor.kind: k8s`, and refuses a live (`dryRun: false`) render without it.\n559:- **Observability.** Every claim, close-out and failure is an event in local state. A daily digest on the pinned status issue shows queue depth by label, jobs run, failures and spend. Each issue's body also carries a per-job cost table (agent, tokens by type, cost, model, outcome and a link to the job's comment, PR) with a Total row, written by the `costs` loop. The digest is a `digest` loop that posts one new comment a day on the status issue, titled \"daily digest for <date> (UTC)\", covering the previous complete UTC day: open-issue counts per `ard:` label, jobs run, failures (with a per-role split) and spend. A `digest` event written after the post is the once-per-day marker, so a restart does not repost; it holds numbers, label and role names only. A reached daily cap is a `budget_capped` event. The repository may be public, so the spend it shows is public too; the table holds no agent text or host paths. Scanner failure streaks (#221, the early slice of the digest, #58): each scanner posts one comment on the status issue when a failure streak starts and one when it recovers. A streak starts at the first run that is a guard trip or guard error (reason: the guard rule name or `scan-error`), any other `scan_failed` run (a `timeout` counts as `scan_failed`) or a skip at `max_open`, and ends at the next `ok` run. The posted class is kept in the scanner's `meta` table (key `failure_streak`), so a restart does not repost, and it is written only after the post succeeds. Runs dropped at the global cap and `error` runs neither start nor end a streak. A comment carries the scanner name and the reason class only, never finding text, titles, fingerprints or log lines. The post goes through the effect layer, so a dry-run logs it (`scanner.streak_notice`) and stores no streak. While the status issue number is unknown nothing is posted and the streak is not recorded, so it posts once the issue exists. Command center (#414): when stdin and stdout are both a terminal and `TERM` is not `dumb`, `ard serve` draws a read-only terminal UI (Bubble Tea) instead of streaming logs; `--ui` forces it (and fails when there is no terminal), `--no-ui`, a pipe or `TERM=dumb` keep today's output unchanged. While it runs, all log output and child stderr go to `~/.ard/logs/<owner>/<repo>/serve.log` (mode 0600, rotated to `serve.log.1` at 10 MiB); the header shows the path and it is printed on exit. Once a second the UI redraws panels from the in-process Snapshot (needs you, running, waiting per role, limits, issues paged, and on large terminals next tick per loop and recent events) and makes no GitHub call. Keys: `?` help, `j`/`k` or arrows select, `o` opens the selected issue in the browser, `p` pauses or unpauses (local pause file), `+` / `-` change the slot cap, `q` quits after a confirm, Ctrl-C stops gracefully. Every string it draws (titles, agent text) is stripped of control characters and escape sequences first, and no secret is shown.\n589:- **Role images ([#245](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/245)).** Owner decisions: role `Dockerfile`s are built **in the cluster** when a job needs one, not in CI; the images are **public** on ghcr as `<roleImages.repository>/<type>-<name>` (default `ghcr.io/albertcbrown/ard/<type>-<name>`), so job pods pull them with no `imagePullSecrets`; a role image built on the agent image starts `FROM ghcr.io/albertcbrown/ard/agent:latest`, so `ard-build` must also move the agent image's `:latest` tag. The chart side (#448, off by default): with `roleImages.enabled` and executor k8s, `deploy/charts/ard-serve` creates the build namespace `ard-build-<instance>`, apart from the jobs namespace because builder pods hold the push credential. Rootless BuildKit (`roleImages.builder.image`, pinned by digest) needs seccomp and AppArmor Unconfined, which Pod Security restricted and baseline forbid, so that namespace enforces privileged and warns and audits at baseline; a ValidatingAdmissionPolicy admits only the builder shape instead: the `ard-build` ServiceAccount with no token, exactly one `buildkit` container running that exact image as `roleImages.builder.uid`, not privileged and adding no capabilities, no host namespaces, image pull secrets or envFrom, and Secret volumes limited to the push credential and the pod's own Secret. Builder pods reach DNS and the internet minus the private ranges, and nothing reaches them. The push credential is a `kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson` Secret, optionally built by an ExternalSecret from one item's `username` and `token` (a classic PAT with `write:packages`; ghcr refuses App and fine-grained tokens). The controller gets the build namespace, builder image and uid, role image repository and the push Secret's name as `ARD_K8S_BUILD_NAMESPACE`, `ARD_K8S_BUILDER_IMAGE`, `ARD_K8S_BUILDER_UID`, `ARD_K8S_ROLE_IMAGE_REPOSITORY` and `ARD_K8S_ROLE_IMAGE_PUSH_SECRET`, never the credential, and a Role in the build namespace like the jobs one. **Residual risks:** the controller's token can create builder pods that mount the push credential, so a stolen controller token can push to the role image packages (the admission policy limits it to the pinned BuildKit image, not to a given build); and with a `:latest` base, a role image tagged by its role directory keeps an older agent base until the role changes. Each package must be made public once after its first push, and AGPL roles (`trufflehog`) stay off under k8s so they are never published. Runbook: [docs/runbooks/role-images.md](runbooks/role-images.md).\n613:**One `ard serve` per repository.** To run ard on another repository, start a second `ard serve` for it; limits and credentials belong to each process (see Configuration). The steps are in [the onboarding runbook](runbooks/onboard-repo.md).\n9:test:\n12:test-race:\n15:lint:\n62:lint-scanner-fixtures:","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"748a3d80-7d7b-4b04-ada8-d4203eb53dab","uuid":"003e9f55-e12d-4654-8fd1-c507a8ac5050","timestamp":"2026-11-26T19:02:08.316Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"133:\tAgent   string        // role > global\n167:\t// Budget caps (#8): zero means unset — the job cap falls back to\n168:\t// Resolved.MaxBudgetUSD and the daily cap to Resolved.BudgetUSDPerDay.\n169:\tMaxBudgetUSD    float64\n170:\tBudgetUSDPerDay float64\n214:\tAgent           string  // default agent CLI\n216:\tBudgetUSDPerDay float64 // whole-repo daily cap; default 50\n217:\tMaxBudgetUSD    float64 // default per-job cap; default 5\n244:// RoleBudgetUSDPerDay is the role's own daily cap in USD. set is false when\n246:// only the repo-wide Resolved.BudgetUSDPerDay applies to it.\ninternal/config/raw.go:27:\tintervals              rawIntervals\ninternal/config/raw.go:42:// rawIntervals keeps every interval optional so defaults can tell \"unset\"\ninternal/config/raw.go:44:type rawIntervals struct {\ninternal/config/raw.go:126:\t\tcase \"intervals\":\ninternal/config/raw.go:127:\t\t\terrs = append(errs, raw.intervals.parse(value, cfgPath, \"intervals\")...)\ninternal/config/raw.go:163:func (ri *rawIntervals) parse(n *yaml.Node, file, prefix string) Errors {\ninternal/config/load.go:197:\t\trole.Every = time.Duration(res.Intervals.Scanners)\ninternal/config/types.go:38:// Intervals holds the poll interval of every loop. Durations under one\ninternal/config/types.go:41:type Intervals struct {\ninternal/config/types.go:140:\tEvery     time.Duration // default Intervals.Scanners\n88:**One query per role per poll.** For example: `is:open label:\"ard:triage:architect\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:running\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:done\" -label:ard:executor:failed -label:ard:needs-human -label:ard:hold`. Six triage roles every 15 minutes, plus four reviewers, a worker and the merger every 5, is about 100 searches an hour, well under GitHub's limits.\n207:**The LLM engine** (`scanner.AgentEngine`, #204) runs the role's agent as a `scanner`-stage job (Issue 0) on a read-only worktree at the scanned commit, with the role's sidecar, a writable output dir (`$ARD_OUT`) and the model credential only; the whole repository and the sidecar are untrusted input. With no model credential it asks for no slot and runs nothing. The agent writes its findings to `$ARD_OUT/findings.jsonl`, which must be a regular file (never a symlink) of at most 16 MiB; it is decoded with the strict schema above, then checked against the fingerprint grammar, and a missing file is a failed run. Usage is recorded before any outcome check, so a failed run keeps its cost. A usage-limit stop is recorded in the limits book and the job ends as limited; if the book cannot record it, the run fails instead. A timeout, a vanished process, a non-zero exit, an unsuccessful result or an invalid findings file is a failed scan whose error carries the exit code and result subtype, or line numbers, and never agent text. The engine does no redaction itself: its decoded findings pass the same pre-filing guard as every scanner's (below), so a secret hidden behind a JSON escape in the file is still caught, and only after the guard passes are `@`-mentions in titles and bodies defused (`closeout.Sanitize`: a zero-width space after `@`, comment openers escaped), so an inserted character can never split a secret pattern the guard should see.\n328:Kubernetes-executor details (decided, design revision 3 of [#54](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/54), recorded in [#312](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/312); the work is split across later parts of #54, and #317 splits into the API layer and the executor). There is no job API on `ard serve`. **Pod shape.** `pre` fetches with the token passed through `http.extraheader` from the environment, never argv or `.git/config`; the git dir is on a `ctl` volume the agent does not mount; the role dir comes from `cfg.SHA`, so the k8s executor refuses a config without a SHA; sidecars are at pinned SHAs; the untrusted inputs and the scrub list travel in the per-job Secret, capped at 768 KiB (over the cap is a permanent failure with a named cause); `pre` copies the static `ard` binary. The `agent` container mounts only `work` and `out` and holds only its model credential via `secretKeyRef`: no GitHub token, no key, no Secret volume, no SA token. It tees stdout to `out/stdout`, enforces its own deadline, writes a done marker and reaps orphans as PID 1. `post` has its own pinned image and never runs a binary from a shared volume. It serves one route, `GET /v1/result`, on the pod IP, guarded by a preshared 32-byte `crypto/rand` key unique to the pod, mounted only in `post` and kept in memory by the controller, compared in constant time. It answers 409 until the agent has terminated and the marker exists, builds the tar in Go and never runs git, serves one request at a time, has header and idle timeouts, never logs Authorization, and exits 0 after the controller confirms or at the deadline. The transport is plain HTTP inside the cluster (the owner's choice): the key grants only this job's result, and a NetworkPolicy limits who can connect.\n334:**Layout and limits.** Pod paths are `/job`, `/work`, `/role`, `/sidecar`, `/ard-config`, `/ard-out` and `/ard-bin`; the result roots are `work/` and `out/`, and stdout is `out/stdout`, outside `work/`, so it never shows in `Snapshot.Changed`. Defaults: a 2 GiB `emptyDir` per pod, a result of 512 MiB compressed and 2 GiB unpacked, 200k files, 100 MiB per file, all overridable by `ard serve` flags. **Deadlines.** `h.Deadline = Start + Timeout`; the job-exec deadline is `h.Deadline - min(2m, Timeout/10)`; the pod's `activeDeadlineSeconds` is `Timeout + UploadGrace`, where `UploadGrace` is a 2 minute result-pull grace; the key is revoked at `Deadline` plus that grace; job-exec exits 124 at its deadline, which maps to `TimedOut`. **Settings and images.** Cluster settings (jobs namespace, images, sizes) are `ard serve` flags set by the chart, not `.ard/config.yaml`; `executor` is fixed at startup, and a later config with a different value is a config error until restart. The `ard-agent` image will be public under `ghcr.io/albertcbrown/ard` and holds no secrets (an image test checks it). It carries Go, jq, yq, osv-scanner, govulncheck and golangci-lint at the repo's pins but not trufflehog (AGPL). Roles with a `Dockerfile` use it until in-cluster builds exist.\n336:**Agent CLIs plug in through adapters.** Each adapter turns (prompt file, context paths, budget) into a command line and parses the exit code and, where the CLI reports them, turns and cost. For Claude Code that is `claude -p --output-format=stream-json --verbose --restricted --safe-mode --strict-mcp-config --disable-slash-commands --permission-mode=dontAsk --settings='{\"includeGitInstructions\":false,\"disableAllHooks\":true}' --tools=… --allowed-tools=… --disallowed-tools=… --model <model> --max-budget-usd <max_budget_usd> --append-system-prompt-file <role prompt> -- <message>` (`--verbose` is required by `stream-json` in print mode). Every stage (scanner, triage, worker, review) gets the same tool profile: `--tools` and `--allowed-tools` are `Read,Glob,Grep,Edit,Write,Bash`, and `--disallowed-tools` is `Bash(git commit *),Bash(git push *),Bash(git remote *),Bash(gh *)` (decided, #79). ard does not pass `--bare`: it limits the CLI to Bash, Read and Edit, and `--tools` cannot widen it (#105). The planted-config isolation `--bare` gave comes instead from `--restricted` (only managed settings and `--settings` load), `--safe-mode` (no CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md, skills, agents, hooks, MCP servers or auto memory), `--strict-mcp-config`, `--disable-slash-commands`, and `--settings` that turns off hooks and the git status snapshot (commit subjects are third-party text). `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_BACKGROUND_TASKS=1` is set as a fixed env var (the executor allows it) so Bash cannot detach commands. The integration tests pin each of these against a worktree with planted config and must be re-run on every `TestedVersion` bump. The tool list is not a security boundary, and the deny list is advisory, because shell indirection gets around it. The real boundaries are the missing GitHub token (Docker; see the host-mode note above, #84), the committer, which commits only `.ard/<issue#>/` from a triage or review job (other edits are discarded with the worktree), and the diff guard on worker changes. An explicit tool list from the caller (`Spec.Tools`) replaces the allow list and keeps the deny list. The user message holds only fixed text and the paths of the untrusted input files. The scanner stage (an LLM scanner, #204) has no issue (`Spec.Issue` is 0) and no untrusted file list: its message says that everything it can read, the repository in the working directory and any sidecar dir, is untrusted data, and asks for findings as JSON lines in `Spec.OutputFile` (`$ARD_OUT/findings.jsonl`), which is required for that stage and refused for every other. Because the CLI's Write and Edit tools reach only the working directory and the `--add-dir` dirs, and a scanner's worktree is read-only, the adapter adds `--add-dir=<dir of OutputFile>` after the read dirs; an integration test pins that a Write elsewhere fails or is denied. Bash is not confined by it: the scanner stage keeps the standard tool profile, Bash included. The installed CLI accepts an undocumented `--max-turns`; ard does not rely on it, so a per-job dollar cap and the executor's hard timeout bound each run. Agents run with an empty per-job CLI config dir and HOME and receive exactly one model credential, passed by name by the executor and never visible to the adapter: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) or a subscription OAuth token (`CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN`, from `claude setup-token`); that rests on the environment, not on a CLI flag. `ard serve` chooses the credential from its own environment: `ARD_AGENT_AUTH` is `auto` (the default; the token if set, else the key), `oauth` or `apikey`, and an explicit mode never falls back to the other variable. Only one is ever passed, because the CLI prefers the API key and would silently bill it (pinned by an integration test). A missing credential does not stop startup, because deterministic scanners need none: `ard serve` logs one INFO line, and a job that needs one fails with a clear error. The executor creates the per-job config dir as `<job home>/.claude` (0700) in `prepare` (`Handle.View.ConfigDir`; under Docker it is a bind mount at `/ard-config`), refuses to start a job whose config dir is not that empty directory, and removes it with the job home; the Reaper's `KeepWorktree` retention keeps it too, and it holds no credential, since the credential travels only in the environment. `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBPROCESS_ENV_SCRUB=1`, which would keep the token out of Bash children, is not set (decided, #125). Residual risk: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) is inherited by Bash children; on the tested release the CLI does not pass `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` to them; and either credential can still be read from the `claude` process's own environment (`/proc/$PPID/environ` on Linux, `ps eww` on macOS). The container is the boundary: it holds no GitHub token, and the credential is scoped to the model. The scrub is off because on 2.1.328 it requires bubblewrap (and socat) plus unprivileged user namespaces; Docker blocks those under ard's flags (`--cap-drop ALL`, `no-new-privileges`, default seccomp), so every Bash call would fail closed and without bwrap the CLI refuses to start. On macOS host mode it worked and matched the `dontAsk` outcomes. It can be revisited for host mode or a loosened container. What agent Bash can read differs by mode: under Docker, only the job's own credential; in host mode, far more, because any same-uid process's environment is readable (`ps eww <pid>` on macOS, `/proc/<pid>/environ` on Linux), including `ard serve`'s own, which holds both credentials. The scrub would not close that; it is part of the host-mode risk of #84. `kimi` and `dsh-tui` both run headless with `-p`; neither has a turn cap and `kimi` reports no cost, so turns and cost are optional in the adapter result. `config.yaml` names the default CLI and any per-role override, so a role can move between CLIs without touching its prompt. The Claude adapter also reads a usage limit and token usage from the stream: a limit comes from a `rate_limit_event` with status `rejected` (its `resetsAt` is the reset time), from the CLI's own error text (`You've hit your session, weekly, Opus or Sonnet limit`) and from `rate_limit` or `billing_error` errors, and it records `usage` and `modelUsage` token counts (by input, output, cache write and cache read; per model when the result has `modelUsage`, which is preferred for accounting). That text is matched only on paths the CLI itself writes (an assistant message carrying an `error`, or an errored result with HTTP 429 or `api_error`), never in agent output, because an agent can be made to print the phrase. Only the job's own agent step and its fix steps are read for a limit: a worker's test step runs agent-written code, so its output is never classified (a printed limit line there cannot block the account). With an API key a 429 stays a transient `rate_limit` retry; only subscription (OAuth) runs have usage limits. The adapter also reads `rate_limit_info.unifiedWindows` from every `rate_limit_event` into `Result.Windows` (one reading per window type, the last winning, also when the run ends with no result). The controller records the readings for OAuth steps only, from the job's own agent and fix steps and never from a test step, including killed steps with no result; it logs them as `limits.reading`. A recording error is logged as `limits.observe_failed` and never fails the job. Nothing is gated on the readings yet (#63). The committed fixtures are scrubbed real captures where a human has made them (`testdata/CAPTURE.md`) and clearly named `synthetic_*` files elsewhere.\n455:**Limits and credentials are per `ard serve`.** `max_pods`, `budget_usd_per_day` and `max_budget_usd` apply to one process. ard does not coordinate across processes, so when a host or cluster runs more than one `ard serve`, the operator sets every serve's limits together so the total fits the host, the cluster and the model subscription. Each `ard serve` has its own GitHub credentials: a GitHub App installation, or a fine-grained PAT after #302. See [the onboarding runbook](runbooks/onboard-repo.md).\n543:- **Cluster.** The jobs namespace has Pod Security `restricted`; the job SA has no roles and no token; `enableServiceLinks: false`, non-root, drop ALL, RuntimeDefault seccomp, read-only root fs, `/tmp` on an emptyDir, `restartPolicy: Never`, images pinned by digest. The controller Role is namespaced and lists exactly the calls the executor makes: pods create/get/list/delete, pods/log get, secrets create/delete; no watch (status is polled), no events, no pods/exec, no Secret reads. The controller gets a projected API token that expires after an hour; the chart creates the namespace, its policies, quota and limits, the Role and the admission policy only with `executor.kind: k8s`, and refuses a live (`dryRun: false`) render without it.\n559:- **Observability.** Every claim, close-out and failure is an event in local state. A daily digest on the pinned status issue shows queue depth by label, jobs run, failures and spend. Each issue's body also carries a per-job cost table (agent, tokens by type, cost, model, outcome and a link to the job's comment, PR) with a Total row, written by the `costs` loop. The digest is a `digest` loop that posts one new comment a day on the status issue, titled \"daily digest for <date> (UTC)\", covering the previous complete UTC day: open-issue counts per `ard:` label, jobs run, failures (with a per-role split) and spend. A `digest` event written after the post is the once-per-day marker, so a restart does not repost; it holds numbers, label and role names only. A reached daily cap is a `budget_capped` event. The repository may be public, so the spend it shows is public too; the table holds no agent text or host paths. Scanner failure streaks (#221, the early slice of the digest, #58): each scanner posts one comment on the status issue when a failure streak starts and one when it recovers. A streak starts at the first run that is a guard trip or guard error (reason: the guard rule name or `scan-error`), any other `scan_failed` run (a `timeout` counts as `scan_failed`) or a skip at `max_open`, and ends at the next `ok` run. The posted class is kept in the scanner's `meta` table (key `failure_streak`), so a restart does not repost, and it is written only after the post succeeds. Runs dropped at the global cap and `error` runs neither start nor end a streak. A comment carries the scanner name and the reason class only, never finding text, titles, fingerprints or log lines. The post goes through the effect layer, so a dry-run logs it (`scanner.streak_notice`) and stores no streak. While the status issue number is unknown nothing is posted and the streak is not recorded, so it posts once the issue exists. Command center (#414): when stdin and stdout are both a terminal and `TERM` is not `dumb`, `ard serve` draws a read-only terminal UI (Bubble Tea) instead of streaming logs; `--ui` forces it (and fails when there is no terminal), `--no-ui`, a pipe or `TERM=dumb` keep today's output unchanged. While it runs, all log output and child stderr go to `~/.ard/logs/<owner>/<repo>/serve.log` (mode 0600, rotated to `serve.log.1` at 10 MiB); the header shows the path and it is printed on exit. Once a second the UI redraws panels from the in-process Snapshot (needs you, running, waiting per role, limits, issues paged, and on large terminals next tick per loop and recent events) and makes no GitHub call. Keys: `?` help, `j`/`k` or arrows select, `o` opens the selected issue in the browser, `p` pauses or unpauses (local pause file), `+` / `-` change the slot cap, `q` quits after a confirm, Ctrl-C stops gracefully. Every string it draws (titles, agent text) is stripped of control characters and escape sequences first, and no secret is shown.\n589:- **Role images ([#245](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/245)).** Owner decisions: role `Dockerfile`s are built **in the cluster** when a job needs one, not in CI; the images are **public** on ghcr as `<roleImages.repository>/<type>-<name>` (default `ghcr.io/albertcbrown/ard/<type>-<name>`), so job pods pull them with no `imagePullSecrets`; a role image built on the agent image starts `FROM ghcr.io/albertcbrown/ard/agent:latest`, so `ard-build` must also move the agent image's `:latest` tag. The chart side (#448, off by default): with `roleImages.enabled` and executor k8s, `deploy/charts/ard-serve` creates the build namespace `ard-build-<instance>`, apart from the jobs namespace because builder pods hold the push credential. Rootless BuildKit (`roleImages.builder.image`, pinned by digest) needs seccomp and AppArmor Unconfined, which Pod Security restricted and baseline forbid, so that namespace enforces privileged and warns and audits at baseline; a ValidatingAdmissionPolicy admits only the builder shape instead: the `ard-build` ServiceAccount with no token, exactly one `buildkit` container running that exact image as `roleImages.builder.uid`, not privileged and adding no capabilities, no host namespaces, image pull secrets or envFrom, and Secret volumes limited to the push credential and the pod's own Secret. Builder pods reach DNS and the internet minus the private ranges, and nothing reaches them. The push credential is a `kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson` Secret, optionally built by an ExternalSecret from one item's `username` and `token` (a classic PAT with `write:packages`; ghcr refuses App and fine-grained tokens). The controller gets the build namespace, builder image and uid, role image repository and the push Secret's name as `ARD_K8S_BUILD_NAMESPACE`, `ARD_K8S_BUILDER_IMAGE`, `ARD_K8S_BUILDER_UID`, `ARD_K8S_ROLE_IMAGE_REPOSITORY` and `ARD_K8S_ROLE_IMAGE_PUSH_SECRET`, never the credential, and a Role in the build namespace like the jobs one. **Residual risks:** the controller's token can create builder pods that mount the push credential, so a stolen controller token can push to the role image packages (the admission policy limits it to the pinned BuildKit image, not to a given build); and with a `:latest` base, a role image tagged by its role directory keeps an older agent base until the role changes. Each package must be made public once after its first push, and AGPL roles (`trufflehog`) stay off under k8s so they are never published. Runbook: 